 ],
 "literal": "On the dark branch of the jackfruit tree in the common ground, a female monkey / mistaking the sweet-sounding clear drumhead of the tight-bound drum hung by bards / for a fruit, if she taps it, / a male swan rises in response and dances. / On the cloud-crawling Podiyil hill of Ay who wears heroic anklets and armlets, / except for a dancing girl who approaches, / for glorious kings, approaching is rare indeed.",
 "translation": "On the dark branches of the jackfruit tree in the village common, bards have hung their tightly bound drums. Mistaking the sweet-sounding, clear drumhead for a ripe fruit, a female monkey taps on it. Hearing the sound, a male swan rises and dances in response. On such a cloud-crawling Mount Podiyil of Ay, who wears heroic anklets and armlets, none but a dancing girl can approach; for proud, warring kings, approaching it is impossible.",
 "modern": "ஊர் மன்றத்தில் உள்ள பலா மரத்தின் கரிய கிளையில், இரவலர்கள் தொங்கவிட்டுள்ள வாரினால் கட்டப்பட்ட முழவின் இனிய ஓசையுடைய தெளிந்த கண்ணைப் பழம் என்று கருதி பெண் குரங்கு தட்டினால், அதற்கு மாறாக ஆண் அன்னப் பறவை எழுந்து ஆடும். வீரக்கழலும் தோள்வளையும் அணிந்த ஆய் மன்னனின் மேகங்கள் தவழும் பொதிய மலையை, ஆடும் கூத்த மகள் நெருங்க முடியுமே தவிர, பெருமை பொருந்திய பகை மன்னர்கள் நெருங்குவது மிகவும் அரிது.",
 "explanation": "In this poem, the poet Uṟaiyūr Ēṇiccēri Muṭamōciyār praises the chieftain Āy Aṇṭiraṉ by describing the unique nature of his mountain, Mount Podiyil. The poet depicts a peaceful scene where a female monkey taps on a bard's drum, mistaking it for a fruit, which prompts a male swan to dance. This image highlights how welcoming and safe the mountain is for artists, bards, and dancers who seek Ay's patronage. In contrast, the mountain is completely inaccessible to hostile, proud kings who come with military intent. Through this contrast, the poet beautifully illustrates Ay's dual nature of extreme generosity to the peaceful and absolute resistance to the aggressive.",
